WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.422
Coding Exercise 3 combines row filtering and group filtering.

00:00:04.422 --> 00:00:08.852
Use sales. First, keep only source rows where price is at least twenty-five.

00:00:08.852 --> 00:00:12.497
Then group the remaining rows by category.

00:00:12.497 --> 00:00:17.422
Calculate total quantity and total revenue for each category.

00:00:17.422 --> 00:00:23.932
After those groups exist, keep only categories whose total quantity is greater than ten.

00:00:23.932 --> 00:00:26.557
Finally, sort with the highest revenue first.

00:00:26.557 --> 00:00:30.852
Think about the order conceptually before you write the clauses.

00:00:30.852 --> 00:00:34.572
The price condition belongs to individual rows.

00:00:34.572 --> 00:00:37.942
The total-quantity condition belongs to groups.

00:00:37.942 --> 00:00:41.372
Run and Check when you are ready.

00:00:41.372 --> 00:00:48.917
If the result does not match, verify the placement of WHERE and HAVING before changing the aggregate

00:00:48.917 --> 00:00:53.487
expressions. For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:53.487 --> 00:00:58.857
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:58.857 --> 00:01:03.392
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:01:03.392 --> 00:01:08.807
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:01:08.807 --> 00:01:15.867
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:15.867 --> 00:01:20.042
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:01:20.042 --> 00:01:24.748
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
